        } finally {
            kafkaConsumer.resume(kafkaConsumer.assignment());
        }
    }
{code}
And I think this is indeed a problem on the KafkaConsumer side. You can browse the *RootCause Analysis* and *Suggestion* mentioned in Jira.
 Thanks.

Haha,  [~showuon] thank you for your reply and recognition of me:D

 

1. Thank you for your suggestion, and I think it is better to print the relevant prompt log when cleaning the paused mark.

2.
{quote}{{When we execute invokePartitionsRevoked(revokedPartitions), do we consider the need to clean up the messages in KafkaConsumer memory corresponding to revokedPartitions?}}
{quote}
This means that when ConsumerCoordinator#onJoinPrepare(...) is executed, no matter "protocol=EAGER" or "protocol=COOPERATIVE", the invokePartitionsRevoked(revokedPartitions) operation will be executed. In addition, COOPERATIVE will have a "!revokedPartitions.isEmpty()" restriction.

After executing invokePartitionsRevoked(revokedPartitions), I understand that it means that after the rebalance is completed, kafkaConsumer will no longer hold this part of topicPartitions with *a high probability*. So is it necessary for KafkaConsumer to keep the messages of this part of the partition? Especially the instance variable *nextInLineFetch* in Fetcher may contain the message of revokedPartitions.

Can we *try to clean up* the messages in the memory corresponding to revokedPartitions while executing invokePartitionsRevoked(revokedPartitions)?
 In this way, you can avoid that after the paused mark is cleared, Fetcher#fetchedRecords() will return messages for these partitions.

 

3.
{quote}We better support the pause(...) method on the KafkaConsumer side that is not affected by groupRebalance
{quote}
Let's review this code again
{code:java}
        ......

        subscriptions.assignFromSubscribed(assignedPartitions);
        
        //Add new code here
        if (customerPausedPartitions != null && customerPausedPartitions.size() != 0){
            customerPausedPartitions.forEach(topicPartition -> {
                if(subscriptions.isAssigned(topicPartition))
                    subscriptions.pause(topicPartition);
            });
            customerPausedPartitions = null;
        }
{code}
1）My code change is *added after* ”subscriptions.assignFromSubscribed(assignedPartitions)“ , so it will not cause the same partition to be assigned to multiple consumers.
 The purpose of the new code is: if the latest assignment of kafkaConsumer *still holds the topicPartitions* marked paused before rebalance, kafkaConsumer will maintain the paused marking of these partitions

2）If the original paused partitions are allocated to other consumers.

      I also stated in the note: If the new assignment of kafkaConsumer no longer contains topicPartitions that have been paused before rebalance, the paused mark of these topicPartitions will be lost forever on the kafkaConsumer side, even if in a future rebalance, the kafkaConsumer will hold these partitions again.

