h3. What happened

{{LogCompactionTest.test_log_compaction}} is flaky. In the 4.4.0-rc1 system test run (zstd level 10, ISOLATED_KRAFT), the tester read only 7498 of about 191k rows and the validation failed. The same case passed 5 out of 5 times on rerun. The trimmed logs are attached.

The cause is in the tester, not in the broker or the consumer. {{LogCompactionTester.consumeMessages()}} ({{tools/src/main/java/org/apache/kafka/tools/LogCompactionTester.java}}) stops at the first empty {{poll()}}:

{code:java}
while (true) {
    ConsumerRecords<String, String> consumerRecords = consumer.poll(Duration.ofSeconds(20));
    if (consumerRecords.isEmpty()) return consumedFilePath;
    ...
}
{code}

Since KAFKA-12980 (3.2.0), {{poll()}} can return no records even when more data remains. So {{consumeMessages()}} sometimes stops early, the consumed file is incomplete, and {{validateOutput()}} fails. This is not a 4.4 regression: the consumer behavior exists since 3.2.0, and the tester code is even older.

h3. Details

Why {{poll()}} returns no records: after compaction, a batch keeps its {{lastOffset}} even when its last records are removed. If one {{poll()}} returns exactly {{max.poll.records}} records and the last one is the last retained record of a fetch, the next {{poll()}} only moves the position to {{lastOffset + 1}} and returns right away with no records. In the failed run, the tester stopped at a batch with {{lastOffset}} 649433 whose last retained record is 649426. The consumer behavior is expected (KAFKA-14865 tracks it). This ticket only fixes the tester.

Simple repro (offsets 5..9 compacted away; {{fetch.max.bytes=1}} for one batch per fetch, {{max.poll.records=5}}):

{noformat}
poll#1 count=5 position=5  endOffset=16   (offsets 0..4)
poll#2 count=0 position=10 endOffset=16   (skips removed offsets 5..9: empty, but not at the end)
poll#3 count=5 position=15 endOffset=16   (offsets 10..14)
{noformat}

Fix: in {{consumeMessages()}}, get the end offsets before consuming, and poll until the position of every partition reaches its end offset. Give up only when a poll returns no records and moves no position.
